\chapter{Introduction}

\section{Background}

Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems combine information retrieval with language generation. This design solves a problem with purely parametric large language models (LLMs): a model's knowledge is fixed at training time and cannot be updated without retraining or fine-tuning it. \citet{lewis2020rag} introduced RAG to address this problem by combining a pretrained retriever with a pretrained sequence-to-sequence generator, so the model can base its output on external, updatable evidence instead of only on what it learned during training. In a RAG system, a user query is first used to retrieve relevant information from an external knowledge base, typically a vector database. This retrieved information is then given to a language model, together with instructions for generating the final response.

The system prompt connects the intended behavior of the system to its observable output. Unlike the retrieved evidence, which depends on the query and the knowledge base, the system prompt is written on purpose: it specifies how the model should behave, which instructions it should follow, which constraints it should respect, and which output format it should produce. Because the system prompt is written in natural language rather than a formal specification, its effect on behavior is hard to predict and hard to check in advance. This is why its influence on both intended behavior and actual output needs to be studied through controlled experiments.

\section{Motivation and Research Objective}

Understanding how a RAG system responds to its system prompt matters in practice. Whether a system reliably follows its instructions decides whether its behavior can be trusted once deployed, especially when the same retrieval and generation components are reused across many applications with only the system prompt changing. This raises the question of \textit{how systematic changes in the system prompt can be shown to alter the behavior and outcomes of a RAG system.}

At the same time, isolating the effect of the system prompt is hard. Its influence overlaps with the influence of the retrieved evidence, the language model itself, and even the exact wording of the instructions. All of these must be controlled before any change in behavior can be attributed to the system prompt rather than to something else. Prior work on prompt sensitivity in language models has shown that even small, seemingly harmless changes in wording can cause large changes in model behavior (e.g., Salinas and Morstatter 2024; Mu et al. 2025), and that this sensitivity remains even under adversarial or conflicting user input. This is why the present thesis treats the system prompt as a controlled experimental variable, not just a minor setting.

This thesis therefore focuses on one central research question: \textit{To what extent do systematic changes in the system prompt alter the behavior and outcomes of a RAG system when the user input, knowledge base, retrieval mechanism, and underlying language model are held constant?} Answering it requires two things: a formal model of the RAG system precise enough to isolate the system prompt as a controlled parameter, and an experimental design that holds every other source of variation fixed. The rest of this thesis builds both.

\section{Thesis Structure}

This thesis has seven chapters. Chapter 2 covers the background needed for the rest of the work: it traces how retrieval-augmented approaches to language generation developed, and introduces the components of a RAG system used throughout later chapters. Chapter 3 reviews the relevant literature in three parts, prompt sensitivity in language models, retrieval-augmented generation specifically, and adversarial instructions delivered through retrieved content, and identifies the resulting research gap. Chapter 4 builds a formal model of the RAG system used in this thesis: a causal description of the pipeline in which the system prompt is an intervention, a formalization of the documents to be retrieved, and a definition of what counts as a good answer. Chapter 5 describes the experimental setup: how the corpus and query set are generated, and which estimators are used for the criterion defined in Chapter 4. Chapter 6 presents and discusses the results of the experiments. Chapter 7 concludes the thesis by summarizing the findings and outlining directions for future work.

\section*{Notation}

Throughout this thesis, the following notation is used:

\begin{itemize}
    \item $q \in \mathcal{Q}$ denotes a user query, drawn from the space of possible queries $\mathcal{Q}$.
    \item $D = \{d_1, d_2, \ldots, d_N\} \in \mathcal{D}$ denotes the knowledge base, where each $d_i$ is a document, $N$ is the number of documents, and $\mathcal{D}$ is the space of possible knowledge bases.
    \item $S \in \mathcal{S}$ denotes a system prompt, drawn from the space of possible system prompts $\mathcal{S}$.
    \item $C \in \mathcal{C}$ denotes the retrieved context supplied to the language model.
    \item $y \in \mathcal{Y}$ denotes the generated system output, drawn from the space of possible responses $\mathcal{Y}$.
    \item $R: \mathcal{Q} \times \mathcal{D} \rightarrow \mathcal{C}$ denotes the retrieval function, and $G: \mathcal{Q} \times \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{S} \rightarrow \mathcal{Y}$ denotes the generation function.
    \item $\Sigma = \{\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_m\}$ denotes the set of requirements a system prompt states, where each $\sigma_k$ is a property an answer either has or does not have.
    \item $\varphi$ denotes the wording in which those requirements are expressed, and $S = \rho(\Sigma,\varphi)$ denotes the resulting system prompt.
\end{itemize}

\chapter{Preliminaries}

Chapter 1 established that the system prompt is the interface through which a RAG system's intended behavior is specified, and asked how systematically varying this interface changes system behavior and outcomes. Before that question can be answered formally, the components of a RAG system and the reasons behind its design need to be introduced. This chapter covers that background: Section 2.1 traces how retrieval-augmented approaches to language generation developed, Section 2.2 introduces the components of a RAG system used throughout later chapters, and Section 2.3 discusses typical use cases and the failure modes they expose.

\section{Path towards RAG}

The need to store knowledge outside of any single memory, and to retrieve it again on demand, predates computing by a wide margin. Inca administrators recorded census and tax data in knotted cords, quipu, that only specially trained officials could encode or decode, separating the record itself from the person consulting it. Centuries earlier, the Library of Alexandria's cataloger Callimachus organized its holdings by author and subject in the Pinakes, one of the first attempts to make a large external collection searchable rather than merely stored. \citet{bush1945asmaythink} made the need for machine-assisted retrieval explicit, proposing a device that could store a person's library and retrieve from it through associative links between related items rather than a single fixed order. \citet{codd1970relational} gave this idea a rigorous computational form, treating a knowledge store as a formally queryable structure, and \citet{salton1975vectorspace} extended retrieval from exact structured queries to similarity ranking over unstructured text, the direct ancestor of the embedding-based retrieval described later in this chapter.

This same need, a mechanism to retrieve from an external store rather than to remember everything internally, carried directly into question-answering systems, which did not begin with language models either. Early systems relied on hand-crafted rules and pattern matching over the input question, mapping recognized syntactic patterns directly onto retrieval or answer-generation procedures \citep{pandya2021qasurvey}. As statistical and later neural methods replaced these rule-based components, the dominant approach shifted toward learning the mapping from question to answer directly from data, culminating in large language models that generate answers from parameters learned over large text corpora rather than from any explicit rule set.

Large language models generate text using only parametric knowledge, that is, information encoded in their weights during training \citep{wang2024knowledgemechanisms}. This has two problems. First, a model's knowledge is fixed at the point its training data was collected: any event, fact, or document from after that point is unknown to the model unless it is retrained or fine-tuned, which is costly and has to be repeated every time knowledge changes. Second, purely parametric models are prone to hallucination: they can generate fluent, plausible-sounding statements that no evidence actually supports, because nothing in their design forces an output to trace back to a verifiable source.

Both problems are the reason to give a language model access to an external, updatable source of evidence at inference time, instead of relying only on what is stored in its parameters \citep{lin2025llminference}. \citet{lewis2020rag} formalized this idea as Retrieval-Augmented Generation. It combines a pretrained neural retriever, which selects relevant passages from a document collection, with a pretrained sequence-to-sequence generator, which conditions its output on those retrieved passages in addition to the input query. This means the same language model can be paired with different or updated document collections without retraining, and a generated response can be grounded in, and in principle traced back to, specific retrieved evidence.

Since it was introduced, RAG has developed beyond this original design. \citet{gao2023survey} describe three stages of this development: Naive RAG, where retrieval and generation run as one fixed pipeline; Advanced RAG, which adds steps such as query reformulation or re-ranking retrieved passages before generation; and Modular RAG, where retrieval, generation, and other steps are separate, swappable components that can be rearranged for different tasks. In all of these variants, retrieval and generation stay separate steps. This is why this thesis treats retrieval and generation as two distinct functions, so their interaction with the system prompt can be studied on its own, as formalized in Chapter 4.

\section{Components of RAG}

A RAG system has four components: the knowledge base that evidence is retrieved from, the retriever that selects relevant evidence for a given query, the generator that produces a response from the query and the retrieved evidence, and the system prompt that specifies how the generator should behave. This section introduces each component in turn; Chapter 4 treats them formally. Figure~\ref{fig:rag} shows how they fit together and which data passes between them.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{\input{figures/rag_architecture.tex}}
\caption{Architecture of a RAG system, showing the four components introduced in this section. The retrieval stage matches the user query against a vector database and returns the top-$K$ chunks. The generation stage combines those chunks with the user query and the system prompt into a single prompt and produces the answer.}
\label{fig:rag}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Knowledge Base / Vector Database}

The knowledge base $D = \{d_1, d_2, \ldots, d_N\}$ is the collection of documents that evidence is retrieved from. In most modern RAG systems, documents are first split into smaller passages, called chunks. This is needed because a language model can only take in a limited amount of context, and retrieving a whole document would add mostly irrelevant text along with the relevant part. The chunk size is a trade-off: small chunks are precise but may separate a statement from the sentence that qualifies it, while large chunks keep more context but dilute the part that actually matches the query. \citet{chen2024densex} compare retrieval units of different granularity and report that the choice changes both retrieval quality and the quality of the final answer, which makes chunk size a design parameter rather than an implementation detail.

Each chunk is then mapped to a high-dimensional embedding vector by an embedding model, so that chunks with similar meaning end up close together in the embedding space. Representing text as vectors and measuring relatedness by geometric proximity goes back to the vector space model of \citet{salton1975vectorspace}; what changed with neural embeddings is that the vector is learned rather than derived from term counts. Similarity between a query vector and a chunk vector is usually measured by the cosine of the angle between them, which ignores vector length and compares direction only.

These embeddings are stored in a vector database, which supports fast nearest-neighbor search over large collections of chunks. Comparing a query against every stored vector is exact but becomes too slow as the collection grows, so vector databases index the vectors and use approximate nearest-neighbor search, which trades a small loss in recall for a large gain in speed. The most widely used index of this kind is the hierarchical navigable small world graph of \citet{malkov2020hnsw}, which reaches a nearest neighbour by descending through graph layers of decreasing coarseness instead of scanning the collection. Several systems build on such indexes, and \citet{pan2024vectordb} survey how they differ in indexing, query processing and storage; for this section the relevant difference is how they are deployed. FAISS is a library that is embedded directly in an application and gives fine-grained control over the index structure \citep{johnson2021faiss}. Chroma is a lightweight database aimed at local development and small deployments. The \texttt{pgvector} extension adds vector columns and similarity operators to PostgreSQL, which allows vectors to sit next to existing relational data. Pinecone is a managed service in which indexing and scaling are handled by the provider. For the purposes of this thesis the relevant point is that all four answer the same question, namely which stored vectors lie closest to a query vector, so the choice affects latency, operational cost and reproducibility rather than the logic of the pipeline. The indexing step as a whole, in particular the choice of chunk size and embedding model, decides what evidence is even available to the retriever, regardless of how good the retriever itself is.

\subsection{Retriever}

Given a user query $q$ and a knowledge base $D$, the retriever selects the chunks that are relevant for answering $q$. Chapter 4 formalizes this as a function $R: \mathcal{Q} \times \mathcal{D} \rightarrow \mathcal{C}$. Three approaches are common, and they fail in different ways.

\subsubsection{Dense Retrieval}

Dense retrieval embeds the query with the same embedding model used for the knowledge base and ranks chunks by the similarity between the query embedding and each chunk embedding. Because the embedding is learned, two passages that express the same content in different words are placed close together, so a query can match a chunk with which it shares no vocabulary. This is the mechanism the original RAG architecture of \citet{lewis2020rag} relies on. The weakness is the mirror image of the strength: rare identifiers, product codes, names and numbers carry little semantic signal, and a dense retriever may rank a fluent but irrelevant passage above the one chunk that contains the exact string the query asked about.

\subsubsection{Sparse Retrieval}

Sparse retrieval scores chunks by term overlap with the query. The standard method is BM25 \citep{robertson2009bm25}, which weights each matching term by how often it occurs in the chunk, how rare it is across the collection, and how long the chunk is, so that common words contribute little and long chunks are not favoured merely for containing more words. The representation is sparse because a chunk is described by the vocabulary it actually contains rather than by a dense vector. BM25 requires no training and remains a strong baseline, and it handles exactly the cases dense retrieval struggles with, since an exact term match is what it scores. It fails on paraphrase: a query and a chunk that share no terms receive a score of zero regardless of how closely their meanings agree.

\subsubsection{Hybrid Search}

Since the two methods fail on different queries, many systems run both and combine the results. The difficulty is that the two scores are not on a common scale, so they cannot simply be added. The usual solutions are to normalise the scores before combining them with a weight, or to combine the two rankings rather than the scores. Reciprocal rank fusion \citep{cormack2009rrf} is the standard method of the second kind: each chunk is scored by the reciprocal of its rank in each list, so a chunk ranked highly by either method is promoted without the two scores ever being compared directly. \citet{gao2023survey} describe hybrid retrieval as one of the standard refinements distinguishing later RAG systems from the original design.

\subsubsection{Retrieval Depth}

Whichever method is used, the top-$k$ ranked chunks are returned as the retrieved context. Retrieval quality depends on both how many chunks are retrieved and how precise the ranking is: too few chunks risk missing needed evidence, while too many risk adding irrelevant or conflicting information to the generation step. The order of the chunks also matters, since \citet{liu2024lostmiddle} report that answer accuracy depends on where the relevant passage sits within the context. This thesis fixes the retriever, the chunking and $k$ across all experimental conditions, so that changes in the output cannot be traced back to retrieval.

\subsection{Generator}
\label{sec:generator}

The generator is the large language model that produces the final response. A large language model is a neural network trained on a large text corpus to predict the next token in a sequence, and it generates text by autoregressive decoding: producing one token at a time, each conditioned on all previous tokens. Its parameters, written $\theta_{\text{LM}}$ in Chapter 4, encode what the model learned during training. This is often called parametric memory, and it is fixed once training ends.

Within a RAG pipeline the model is not used primarily as a store of facts. It is used as a reasoning and synthesis component that is conditioned on evidence supplied at inference time: it reads the retrieved chunks, selects the parts that answer the query, resolves references between them, and writes the result as fluent text. This division is the point of the architecture. Facts come from the knowledge base, which can be updated without retraining, while the model contributes language competence and the ability to combine several passages into one answer \citep{lewis2020rag}. When the model answers from parametric memory instead of from the retrieved context, the answer may still be fluent and even correct, but it is no longer grounded in evidence that can be checked, which is one route to the unsupported claims documented by \citet{niu2024ragtruth}.

In practice the query, the retrieved context and the system prompt are not passed to the model separately. They are combined, following a fixed template, into a single input sequence, usually with the system prompt placed first so that it shapes how everything after it is read. Chapter 4 formalizes the generator as a function $G: \mathcal{Q} \times \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{S} \rightarrow \mathcal{Y}$. Because the query, context and system prompt together determine the model's input, changing any one of them can change the output, which is why the others must be held fixed if a change in behaviour is to be attributed to the system prompt alone.

\subsubsection{Decoding and the Temperature Parameter}

Generation is not deterministic by default. At each step the model produces a score $z_v$ for every token $v$ in the vocabulary $\mathcal{V}$, and these scores are turned into a probability distribution by the softmax function with a temperature parameter $T > 0$:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:temperature}
p(v) = \frac{\exp(z_v / T)}{\sum_{v' \in \mathcal{V}} \exp(z_{v'} / T)}.
\end{equation}

The temperature rescales the scores before they are normalised, and it controls how much probability mass is left for tokens the model considers less likely \citep{holtzman2020degeneration}. As $T$ approaches zero the distribution concentrates on the highest-scoring token, and decoding becomes effectively deterministic: the same input produces the same output. As $T$ grows the distribution flattens, lower-scoring tokens become reachable, and the output becomes more varied across runs.

For open-ended writing this variety is useful. For a RAG system it is mostly a liability. A token that the model scores as unlikely given the retrieved context is, by construction, one that the evidence does not support well, so raising the temperature raises the chance of producing a statement the retrieved documents do not contain. High temperature also makes the system harder to evaluate, since the same query and the same evidence no longer yield the same answer. RAG systems intended to be faithful to their sources are therefore usually run at a low temperature, trading variety for grounding and reproducibility. This thesis holds $T$ fixed and low across all experimental conditions; the residual randomness that remains at a fixed temperature is the reason each condition is repeated several times in Chapter 5.

\subsection{System Prompt}

The system prompt $S$ specifies the instructions, constraints and output requirements the generator should follow, independent of the specific query or retrieved context. Unlike the query, which changes with every request, and the retrieved context, which changes with the query and the knowledge base, the system prompt is usually fixed across many requests.

Its role in the architecture is easiest to see by asking what happens without it. Retrieval places relevant evidence in the context window, but placing evidence there does not oblige the model to use it. Section~\ref{sec:generator} described the parameters $\theta_{\text{LM}}$ as a store of information absorbed during training, and that store remains available at inference time. A model given a retrieved passage and a question it believes it already knows the answer to may answer from parameters rather than from the passage, producing an assured and fluent response that the retrieved documents do not support. This failure is not signalled to the user, since fluency and confidence are unaffected by whether the underlying claim was grounded; \citet{niu2024ragtruth} document that unsupported statements continue to appear even when relevant evidence has been retrieved and supplied. The system prompt is the last point in the pipeline at which this can be constrained. It is the only component that can state, before generation begins, that the retrieved context is the sole admissible source of fact.

The system prompt accordingly has two distinct functions. The first is to define the operational scope of the system: the role it occupies, the audience it addresses, the register it writes in, and the format its output takes. The second is to install hard constraints on that behaviour, of which the grounding constraint is the most consequential. A grounding constraint restricts the model to the retrieved context and specifies what to do when that context is inadequate, which converts an unanswerable query from a case the model improvises around into a case with a defined response. The two functions differ in kind: scope shapes how an answer is written, whereas a hard constraint determines whether an answer may be given at all. Listing~\ref{lst:prompt} illustrates both in a minimal example.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={A minimal system prompt combining an operational scope with two hard constraints.},label={lst:prompt}]
You are a customer service assistant for a parcel delivery company.

GROUNDING RULE
Answer only from the context provided below. Do not use information
from your training data, even where you are confident that it is
correct.

INSUFFICIENT EVIDENCE
If the context does not contain the answer, reply with exactly:
The provided documents do not contain this information.

FORMAT
Answer in at most three sentences. Cite the identifier of every
document you use, in square brackets.
\end{lstlisting}

The first line fixes the operational scope. The two blocks that follow are hard constraints: one restricts the admissible source of fact, the other defines the response for the case in which no admissible source exists. The final block constrains form rather than content, and belongs to the first function. Each of these instructions is stated in a way that can be checked against an output, which is the property Chapter 4 requires of the requirement set $\Sigma$.

Whether an instruction of this kind is in fact obeyed is a separate question from whether it has been written. Because the system prompt is expressed in natural language rather than as a specification the generator is bound to satisfy, its effect faces the same instruction-following and prompt-sensitivity issues as any other natural-language instruction to a language model, discussed further in Chapter 3. A grounding constraint that is followed for one phrasing and disregarded for another provides weaker protection than its wording suggests. This is why the system prompt, formalized in Chapter 4 as a set of requirements together with a wording, is the object of experimental control in this thesis.

\section{Retrieval-Augmented Generation and Fine-Tuning}
\label{sec:finetuning}

Fine-tuning is the other established way of adapting a general-purpose language model to a specific application, and it is useful to state how it differs from retrieval before the use cases in Section~\ref{sec:usecase} are discussed. Fine-tuning continues training on domain-specific data and changes the model parameters $\theta_{\text{LM}}$, so what is learned is absorbed into the parametric memory described in Section~\ref{sec:generator}. RAG leaves $\theta_{\text{LM}}$ untouched and changes what is placed in the context window at inference time. The two are therefore not competing answers to one question. They act on different parts of the system, and the practical decision is which part a given requirement belongs to: the knowledge the system reasons over, or the way the system reasons and writes \citep{ovadia2024finetuning}.

For knowledge that is new, changing, or proprietary, retrieval is the better fit, for three reasons. The first is cost. Fine-tuning requires a training run over the model's parameters whenever the underlying information changes, whereas adding a document to a knowledge base requires only that the new chunks be embedded and indexed, which is a fraction of the computation and can be done continuously rather than in scheduled retraining cycles \citep{cheerla2025enterpriserag}. The second is data freshness and revocability. A document can be added, corrected, or deleted and takes effect at the next query, while a fact absorbed into $\theta_{\text{LM}}$ cannot be selectively removed without retraining; \citet{wang2024knowledgemechanisms} survey the difficulty of locating and editing individual facts inside model parameters. This matters wherever information is corrected, retracted, or must be deleted on request, and it is one reason retrieval is preferred in regulated settings, where sensitive data can remain in a controlled external store instead of being absorbed into the parameters \citep{he2025biomedicinerag}. The third is auditability. Because a RAG answer is produced from a specific set of retrieved chunks, each claim in the output can in principle be checked against the passage that supports it, and unsupported claims can be identified at the level of the individual statement \citep{niu2024ragtruth}. A fine-tuned model offers no comparable trace: the information is distributed across its parameters, and the model cannot indicate which training example a given assertion came from. Retrieval does not eliminate unsupported claims, as Section~\ref{sec:usecase} discusses, but it does make them detectable.

Fine-tuning remains the correct choice when the requirement concerns behaviour rather than facts \citep{zhou2023lima}. Three cases are typical. The first is specialised vocabulary: in a domain whose terminology, abbreviations, or notation are poorly represented in the training corpus, a model may misread the query or the retrieved evidence itself, and no amount of retrieval repairs a failure that occurs during interpretation. The second is strict output structure. A system that must emit a fixed JSON schema, a proprietary markup or a domain-specific programming language applies that constraint to every request, and a constraint that holds for every request is more reliably and more cheaply placed in the parameters than restated in every prompt. The third is register and tone, where a consistent style is wanted across many interactions rather than described anew each time. In each case what is being adjusted is how the model responds, not what it knows.

Framed this way, the two techniques are complementary rather than exclusive, and production systems often combine them: a model fine-tuned for the vocabulary and output format of its domain, retrieving the facts it reasons over from an external knowledge base. The separation matters for this thesis because behaviour can be controlled through either channel. Fine-tuning writes the desired behaviour into $\theta_{\text{LM}}$; the system prompt requests it in natural language at inference time. The second channel is far cheaper and can be changed at any moment, which is why it is the one most systems rely on, but it is also the one whose effect is hardest to predict, since a natural-language instruction is not a specification with guaranteed semantics. This thesis holds $\theta_{\text{LM}}$ fixed and studies that second channel.

\section{Use-Case of RAG}
\label{sec:usecase}

RAG systems are typically used where the needed knowledge is too large, too specific, or changes too often to be captured reliably by a model's parametric knowledge alone. Concrete deployments illustrate this across several domains: question-answering over enterprise and internal document collections \citep{cheerla2025enterpriserag}, customer support systems that must answer from current product or policy documentation, coding assistants that retrieve from a specific codebase instead of relying on general programming knowledge, legal question-answering over case law and contract repositories \citep{pipitone2024legalbenchrag}, and clinical decision support that grounds its answers in current medical literature and institutional protocols \citep{he2025biomedicinerag}. In each of these cases, RAG is usually preferred over two alternatives. The first is fine-tuning the generator on the target document collection, which Section~\ref{sec:finetuning} discusses: the collections above change continuously, and retraining on each change is neither economical nor traceable. The second is extending the model's context window to include the whole document collection, which avoids retraining but does not scale to large collections and does not, by itself, make sure the model actually attends to the passages that are relevant to the query.

Despite these advantages, RAG does not remove the failure modes of purely parametric generation; it shifts them, and in some cases only partly fixes them. \citet{chen2024rgb} show that current language models tolerate a moderate amount of irrelevant retrieved content reasonably well, but still struggle to reject a query when none of the retrieved evidence is enough to answer it, to combine evidence spread across multiple retrieved chunks, and to stay robust when retrieved evidence is outdated or contradictory. \citet{niu2024ragtruth} further show that language models keep producing hallucinated content even when relevant evidence has been retrieved and given to them in context. This means that grounding a model in retrieved evidence does not, by itself, guarantee that its output stays faithful to that evidence. These failure modes are why the literature review in Chapter 3 and the experimental design in Chapters 4 and 5 build the query set to include directly answerable, multi-hop, conflicting, and insufficient-evidence cases, so the system prompt's effect on exactly these failure modes can be measured.

\section{Advanced RAG Architectures}
\label{sec:advanced}

The architecture described so far retrieves once per query and generates once from what was retrieved. Recent work relaxes both assumptions, and the resulting variants can be grouped by which stage of the pipeline they modify. The following summary is included for completeness; none of these variants is used in this thesis, which studies the standard architecture.

\begin{description}
    \item[Conversational RAG.] Retaining prior turns of a dialogue as state, so that a follow-up question can be resolved against what was already asked and answered. A query such as ``and the second one?'' is meaningless to a retriever on its own, so the conversation history is used either to rewrite it into a standalone query or, as \citet{liu2024chatqa} show, to train a retriever directly on multi-turn data, which reaches comparable accuracy at lower cost than rewriting.
    \item[Pre-retrieval optimisation.] Rewriting or expanding the query before it reaches the retriever. Hypothetical Document Embeddings (HyDE) generate a hypothetical answer to the query and embed that instead of the query itself, on the argument that an answer lies closer in embedding space to the passage containing it than a question does \citep{gao2023hyde}.
    \item[Routing and adaptive retrieval.] Deciding where and how much to retrieve. Where several collections or indexes are available, a routing step can select among them according to what the query is about, rather than searching one combined collection for everything. Adaptive-RAG goes further and varies the depth of retrieval with the difficulty of the query, answering simple questions directly and reserving multi-step retrieval for those that need it \citep{jeong2024adaptiverag}.
    \item[Self-correction and reflection.] Evaluating retrieved evidence before or during generation. Corrective RAG (CRAG) grades each retrieved document, decomposes the ones it keeps into smaller knowledge strips and discards the strips that are not relevant, falling back to a wider search when retrieval fails \citep{yan2024crag}. Self-RAG instead trains the model to decide for itself when to retrieve and to critique its own draft output, so that retrieval and generation interleave rather than run once each \citep{asai2024selfrag}.
    \item[Agentic RAG.] Treating retrieval as one action among several available to an agent. A meta-agent delegates to document-specific or tool-specific agents and combines their results, which turns a fixed pipeline into a workflow whose steps are chosen at run time \citep{singh2025agentic}.
\end{description}

Other work extends the architecture in directions that are orthogonal to this routing taxonomy: knowledge graphs structure the retrieved evidence rather than treating it as a flat set of passages \citep{edge2024graphrag}, and multimodal retrieval covers images, audio and video alongside text \citep{abootorabi2025multimodalrag}. A recent systematic review places these directions within a single trajectory away from single-shot, text-only retrieval \citep{oche2025systematicreview}. Understanding the standard architecture formalized in this thesis remains a prerequisite for all of them: each varies the retrieval function $R$, the generation function $G$, or the number of times they are applied, but none changes the underlying decomposition $F(q,D;S) = G(q,R(q;D);S)$ on which they still depend.

This thesis implements the standard architecture and none of the variants above. The reason is methodological. The research objective is to estimate the causal effect of the system prompt on the output, which Chapter 4 formalizes as the intervention $\mathrm{do}(S = S_j)$ and which is identifiable only as long as the system prompt reaches the answer through a single path. Each of the variants breaks that condition. Under Self-RAG the model decides for itself when to retrieve \citep{asai2024selfrag}, so the system prompt influences which documents are fetched as well as how they are used. Under routing, corrective and agentic designs the prompt shapes the decisions of the router, the retrieval evaluator or the meta-agent, so the retrieved context $C$ ceases to be determined by the query and the corpus alone. The edge $S \rightarrow C$ that Figure~\ref{fig:causal} deliberately omits would then be present, and a measured difference between two prompt conditions could be attributed either to the prompt's effect on generation or to its effect on what was retrieved, with no way to separate the two. The routing policy would operate as a confounding variable, and the isolation of effects on which the experimental design rests would no longer hold.

The standard architecture is therefore the controlled baseline rather than a simplification adopted for convenience. It is the configuration in which the system prompt enters the pipeline at exactly one point, which is the property that makes the intervention identifiable. The direction of inference matters as well: because the variants above add routing, reflection or delegation around the retrieval and generation functions instead of replacing them, an effect established at this level bounds what can be expected once those mechanisms are introduced, whereas an effect measured inside an agentic pipeline could not be decomposed back into a prompt component and a routing component. It is also the configuration most widely deployed in practice, as Section~\ref{sec:usecase} describes, so the result is not restricted to a hypothetical case. Establishing the causal mechanics of prompt interventions at this level is a prerequisite for studying them in any richer pipeline, and extending the design to one of the variants above is left to future work.

\chapter{Literature Review}

Chapter 2 introduced the components of a RAG system. This chapter reviews three areas of literature relevant to studying how the system prompt affects such a system: Section 3.1 covers prompt sensitivity and instruction-following in language models in general, Section 3.2 covers retrieval-augmented generation specifically, its architecture, evaluation, and known failure modes, and Section 3.3 covers adversarial and competing instructions delivered through retrieved content. Section 3.4 brings all three together into the specific research gap this thesis addresses.

\section{Prompt Sensitivity and Instruction-Following in Language Models}

The first branch of literature covers prompt sensitivity and instruction-following in language models in general, without any retrieval component. These studies show that system prompts are a measurable and controllable way of shaping model behavior, and that this mechanism is sensitive to change at several levels, from prompt specificity down to plain rewording.

\citet{cheng2026systemprompts} study how different system prompts affect instruction-tuned language models for code generation, varying prompt specificity, prompting strategy, model, programming language, and decoding settings; their work directly motivates the controlled manipulation of system prompts in this thesis, while the present experiment extends the analysis to a RAG setting and focuses explicitly on system behaviour and system outcomes. \citet{mu2025robustness} investigate the robustness of system prompts as a mechanism for controlling LLM behaviour, particularly when system instructions interact with conflicting or adversarial user inputs, supporting the modelling of the system prompt as a behavioural control variable and motivating measuring whether a system actually follows the intended instructions. \citet{lu2024sensitivity} study prompt sensitivity across different models and tasks, examining how changes in prompt formulation affect model behaviour and output, which is relevant to the present research because it motivates distinguishing changes in the intended instruction from changes caused by the linguistic formulation of that instruction. \citet{zhuo2024prosa} propose methods for measuring and understanding prompt sensitivity across LLMs and tasks, investigating why seemingly small prompt changes can produce different model behaviour, and their work provides methodological support for treating prompt effects as measurable experimental phenomena rather than anecdotal observations.

\citet{salinas2024butterfly} investigate the sensitivity of LLM decisions to small perturbations in prompts, demonstrating that minor changes in prompt construction can sometimes produce substantial changes in model outputs, which motivates the use of controlled, minimal prompt interventions in this thesis. \citet{zheng2024helpful} investigate the effects of assigning different personas or roles through system prompts and examine whether these changes improve model performance; their findings are relevant to the distinction between changes in intended system behaviour and actual improvements in objective system performance. \citet{ying2024intuitive} examine how LLMs respond to conflicting prompts and information, including situations relevant to retrieval-augmented generation, and their work is particularly relevant to the thesis' interest in how system instructions influence a system when retrieved evidence is incomplete, conflicting, or inconsistent with other information. \citet{ruiz2026promptcarefully} experimentally investigate how LLMs respond to explicit behavioural rules provided through prompts and how these rules interact with changing task contingencies, supporting the proposed view of system prompts as specifications of behavioural policies rather than only textual context.

\citet{hua2025flaw} examine prompt sensitivity and show that some apparent differences between prompts can arise from the way model outputs are evaluated rather than from meaningful behavioural differences, which is important for the present research because it motivates using evaluation metrics that distinguish genuine changes in RAG behaviour and outcomes from superficial output variation. \citet{qin2026interactions} investigate prompt sensitivity through interactions between prompts and model inputs, moving beyond simple comparisons of final outputs to analyze how prompt changes affect model behaviour, and their approach is closely related to the present research's goal of developing a more structured account of how system prompts influence an AI system. \citet{sclar2024quantifying} show that language models are highly sensitive to meaning-preserving prompt formatting changes (e.g., delimiters, casing, list vs.\ prose), with performance swings of up to 76 accuracy points on some models despite no change in propositional content, which directly motivates isolating the Format condition ($S_{\theta_3}$) as a distinct linguistic axis in this thesis's experimental design, separate from lexical or syntactic variation.

Together, these studies make two points that shape the experimental design of this thesis. First, system prompts are not incidental configuration details but a measurable behavioral control variable: their effect on a model's output can be isolated and measured given a properly controlled experimental setup \citep{mu2025robustness,ruiz2026promptcarefully}. Second, prompt effects are confounded by several sources of variation at once: the wording of an instruction as distinct from its meaning \citep{lu2024sensitivity,zhuo2024prosa,salinas2024butterfly}, purely formatting-level changes that alter no actual content \citep{sclar2024quantifying}, and the evaluation method used to detect a behavioral difference in the first place \citep{hua2025flaw}. None of these studies, however, look at system prompts inside a retrieval-augmented setting, where system behavior also depends on retrieved evidence that can itself be noisy, conflicting, or insufficient; \citet{ying2024intuitive} is a partial exception, discussed further below.

\section{Retrieval-Augmented Generation: Architecture, Evaluation, and Failure Modes}

The second branch of literature covers retrieval-augmented generation specifically: its architecture, its evaluation, and the failure modes it shows independent of any system-prompt manipulation.

\citet{lewis2020rag} introduce Retrieval-Augmented Generation, combining a pretrained neural retriever with a pretrained sequence-to-sequence generator, and show that conditioning generation on retrieved evidence outperforms purely parametric models on knowledge-intensive tasks; their architecture is the direct basis for the $F(q,D;S)=G(q,R(q;D);S)$ decomposition formalized in Chapter 4 of this thesis. \citet{gao2023survey} survey the development of RAG systems through \textbf{Naive}, \textbf{Advanced}, and \textbf{Modular} paradigms, organizing the field around a tripartite retrieval-generation-augmentation structure; their taxonomy supports treating retrieval and generation as separable functions whose interaction with the system prompt can be studied independently, as this thesis does. Two later developments extend this taxonomy in directions this thesis deliberately does not follow. \citet{edge2024graphrag} restructure the retrieval side into a knowledge graph, grouped into thematic communities and pre-summarized by an LLM, so that a query is answered by traversing entities and community summaries rather than by a flat nearest-neighbor lookup over $D$. \citet{singh2025agentic} and \citet{jin2025searchr1} restructure the generation side into an agent loop, in which the model itself decides when and what to retrieve, calling $R$ repeatedly and adaptively rather than once per query; \citet{jin2025searchr1} train this behaviour directly with reinforcement learning and report large gains over standard RAG baselines. Both directions relax exactly the components Chapter 4 holds fixed, $R$ in the first case and the single-call structure of $G$ in the second. For that reason this thesis retains \citeauthor{gao2023survey}'s simplest, Naive-RAG form: any change in the outcomes measured here can then be attributed to the system prompt, rather than to the added architectural complexity of a graph-structured retriever or an agentic generation loop.

\citet{es2023ragas} propose Ragas, a reference-free evaluation framework for RAG pipelines built around three metrics, \textbf{faithfulness}, \textbf{answer relevance}, and \textbf{context relevance}, that require no ground-truth human annotation; their faithfulness metric is closely related to the evidence-faithfulness measure used in this thesis, and motivates evaluating the effect of the system prompt independently of retrieval quality. \citet{chen2024rgb} construct the Retrieval-Augmented Generation Benchmark (RGB) and show that current language models tolerate a moderate amount of retrieval noise but struggle with negative rejection, information integration across multiple retrieved passages, and robustness to counterfactual or contradictory evidence; their four testbeds correspond closely to the directly-answerable, multi-hop, conflicting, and insufficient-evidence query categories used in the experimental design of this thesis. \citet{niu2024ragtruth} introduce RAGTruth, a manually annotated corpus of word-level hallucinations produced by RAG systems, and benchmark hallucination-detection methods across multiple language models; their finding that hallucination persists even when relevant evidence has been retrieved motivates measuring hallucination rate as an outcome distinct from retrieval quality, as this thesis does.

Together, these studies establish the architecture, evaluation vocabulary, and known failure modes of RAG systems specifically. They show that a RAG system's output quality depends on both retrieval quality and how faithfully the generator uses the retrieved evidence \citep{es2023ragas,niu2024ragtruth}, and that current systems already struggle with certain evidence conditions, such as conflicting or insufficient retrieved evidence, even without any prompt manipulation \citep{chen2024rgb}. None of the studies in this branch, however, treat the system prompt as a controlled experimental variable; it is either fixed across conditions or not specified at all.

\section{Adversarial and Competing Instructions in Retrieved Content}

A third, closely related line of work studies what happens when instructions arrive not from the system prompt but from the retrieved content itself, competing with the system prompt for control of the model's behavior. Where the first branch shows that a model's own system prompt can be perturbed and still change behavior \citep{mu2025robustness,salinas2024butterfly}, \citet{greshake2023notwhat} show that an entirely different source of instruction, text hidden inside a document the model is told to process, can override the system prompt altogether. This is the adversarial extreme of the same instruction-competition problem, except the competing instruction arrives through the retrieved context $C$ rather than through $S$, a source this thesis deliberately holds fixed rather than varies. \citet{zhang2025poisoning} extend this observation into a RAG-specific benchmark, testing 13 poisoning attack methods and 7 defenses across 5 base question-answering datasets and 10 expanded variants; their findings sit alongside the observations of \citet{chen2024rgb} and \citet{niu2024ragtruth} from the second branch that retrieved evidence can already be noisy, incomplete, or contradictory, and show that it can also be made deliberately adversarial.

\medskip
Both studies, however, treat instruction competition exclusively as a security problem: an adversary deliberately crafts malicious content, and the goal is to detect or defend against it. Neither examines benign, non-adversarial linguistic variation of the system prompt itself, of the kind the prompt-sensitivity literature reviewed above studies outside of RAG, and of the kind this thesis's five conditions ($S_{\theta_0}$ through $S_{\theta_4}$) are built to isolate inside it. This distinction matters: this thesis is not concerned with whether a RAG system can be attacked, but with whether ordinary, well-intentioned rewordings of its own instructions change its behavior at all.

\section{Synthesis and Research Gap}

The first branch shows that system prompts are a measurable, controllable way of shaping language model behavior, sensitive even to small changes in wording, but studies this mostly outside of any retrieval-augmented setting. The second branch establishes the architecture, evaluation vocabulary, and known failure modes of RAG systems, including known problems with evidence integration, rejection, and faithfulness, but does not treat the system prompt as a controlled experimental variable. The third branch shows that instructions competing with the system prompt is a real and actively studied phenomenon in RAG systems, but only as a deliberate security threat delivered through retrieved content, never as benign, controlled linguistic variation of the system prompt itself. The overlap between all three branches is still largely unexamined. \citet{ying2024intuitive} is a partial exception: it looks at how LLMs respond to conflicting prompts and information in retrieval-relevant settings, but without holding retrieval and the underlying language model fixed as an explicit experimental control.

This thesis addresses that gap directly. It treats the system prompt as a controlled experimental variable inside a RAG pipeline, varying only its wording rather than its intent, while holding the query, knowledge base, retrieval mechanism, and underlying language model fixed, so that any change in system behavior and outcomes can be traced to the system prompt's wording alone. Chapter 4 builds the formal model that makes this control precise, describing the RAG system as a retrieval function combined with a generation function, with the system prompt as an explicit parameter of the generation function.

\chapter{Formal Model of a RAG System}

Chapter 3 reviewed literature on prompt sensitivity in language models, on retrieval-augmented generation specifically, and on adversarial instructions delivered through retrieved content, and identified a research gap: the overlap between these branches is still largely unexamined, with existing work such as \citet{ying2024intuitive} addressing conflicting prompts and information in retrieval-relevant settings only, without holding retrieval and the underlying language model fixed as an explicit experimental control. Closing this gap needs a model of the RAG system precise enough to isolate the system prompt as a parameter that can be varied on its own. This chapter builds that model, based on the components introduced descriptively in Chapter 2: the knowledge base $D$, the retriever, the generator, and the system prompt $S$.

Recall from the Notation section of Chapter 1 that $q \in \mathcal{Q}$ denotes a user query, $D = \{d_1, \ldots, d_N\} \in \mathcal{D}$ denotes the knowledge base, $S \in \mathcal{S}$ denotes a system prompt, $C \in \mathcal{C}$ denotes the retrieved context, and $y \in \mathcal{Y}$ denotes the generated output. A simple RAG system, without a system prompt, can be represented by the sequence
\[
q \longrightarrow C \longrightarrow y.
\]
The system prompt influences the generation stage:
\[
(q, C, S) \longrightarrow y.
\]
The complete system can therefore be represented as
\[
F(q, D; S) = G(q, R(q; D); S),
\]
where $R$ is the retrieval function and $G$ is the generation function, both introduced descriptively in Chapter 2 and formalized below.

\subsection*{Retrieval Function}

The retriever is defined as a function
\[
R: \mathcal{Q} \times \mathcal{D} \rightarrow \mathcal{C},
\]
where $\mathcal{D}$ denotes the space of knowledge bases and $\mathcal{C}$ denotes the space of retrieved contexts. For a query $q$ and knowledge base $D$,
\[
C = R(q; D).
\]
The retrieved context may consist of the top-$k$ documents according to a relevance function $r(q,d) \in \mathbb{R}$, where $k \in \mathbb{N}$ is the number of documents retrieved and $d \in D$ ranges over the documents in the knowledge base:
\[
C = R_k(q; D) = \operatorname{TopK}_{d \in D} r(q, d).
\]
Each element of $\mathcal{C}$ is realized as the concatenated text of the retrieved documents, in ranked order, so that $C$ can be inserted directly into the generator's input sequence. The precise implementation of $r$ is not the primary object of this thesis; it is therefore fixed throughout the experiment. This allows changes in system outcomes to be attributed primarily to changes in the system prompt.

\subsection*{Generation Function}

The generator is defined as
\[
G: \mathcal{Q} \times \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{S} \rightarrow \mathcal{Y}.
\]
Given a user query $q$, retrieved context $C$, and system prompt $S$, the generator produces
\[
y = G(q, C; S).
\]
The generator includes the underlying language model and the prompt construction required to supply the model with the user query, retrieved context, and system instructions, as described in Chapter 2. The complete RAG system is therefore
\[
F(q, D; S) = G\left(q, R(q; D); S\right)
\]
This representation is as minimal as possible:
\[
\text{Input} \rightarrow \text{Retrieval} \rightarrow \text{Generation} \rightarrow \text{Output}
\]
with the system prompt as a controlled parameter of generation.

\subsection*{Controlled Variables}

This thesis estimates the effect of the system prompt $S$ on the output. The following variables are held constant across experimental conditions:
\[
q, \quad D, \quad R, \quad G.
\]
Holding $G$ constant also means holding the generator's decoding settings constant: the model checkpoint, decoding strategy, temperature, and any other sampling parameters. Repeated generations under the same condition then vary only because decoding is still random at a fixed setting, not because $G$ itself changed (Chapter 5).

The experimental treatment is the system prompt:
\[
S = S_j,
\]
where $j$ indexes one prompt condition. In the notation introduced below (\S~Modeling the System Prompt), $S_j$ is the same as $S_{\theta_j}$; the two are used interchangeably from here on, with $S_0$ short for the baseline condition $S_{\theta_0}$. For a fixed query and knowledge base,
\[
y_j = F(q, D; S_j).
\]
The comparison between $y_i$ and $y_j$ shows the observed effect of changing the system prompt while keeping everything else fixed.

\subsection*{The Space of System Prompts}

A system prompt does two separable things. It states what the answer must do, and it states this in particular words. Modeling the space $\mathcal{S}$ of possible system prompts amounts to describing these two parts and how they combine.

The first part is a finite set of requirements,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:spec}
\Sigma = \{\sigma_1, \sigma_2, \ldots, \sigma_m\},
\end{equation}

where each $\sigma_k$ is a property that an answer either has or does not have, so that $\sigma_k(y) \in \{0,1\}$ can be checked on any output the system produces. Typical requirements are that the answer use only the retrieved documents, that it say so when those documents do not contain the answer, or that it stay within a given length. Requiring each $\sigma_k$ to be checkable is what later allows adherence to be measured rather than judged impressionistically.

The second part is the wording $\varphi$ in which the requirements are expressed. Four aspects of the wording can be changed without changing which requirements are stated: the words chosen, the sentence structure, the layout, and the strength of the phrasing. A system prompt is then the result of writing the requirements $\Sigma$ in the wording $\varphi$:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:prompt}
\boxed{\;S \;=\; \rho(\Sigma, \varphi).\;}
\end{equation}

Equation~\eqref{eq:prompt} is what makes the experiment possible to state precisely. Two prompts built from the same $\Sigma$ ask for the same thing, whatever they look like; two built from the same $\varphi$ say things the same way, whatever they ask for. The question of this thesis is whether the system is sensitive to $\varphi$ when $\Sigma$ is held fixed.

\subsubsection*{Variation in System Prompts}

Five prompt conditions are used. The first four keep the requirements $\Sigma$ fixed and change only the wording; the fifth changes the requirements themselves.

\begin{description}
    \item[$S_0 = \rho(\Sigma, \varphi_0)$ --- Canonical.] Plain imperative prose. The reference form.
    \item[$S_1 = \rho(\Sigma, \varphi_1)$ --- Lexical.] Synonym substitution, register held fixed. No syntactic change.
    \item[$S_2 = \rho(\Sigma, \varphi_2)$ --- Syntactic.] Clause reordering, active $\leftrightarrow$ passive, imperative $\leftrightarrow$ declarative. Lexicon held as close as possible.
    \item[$S_3 = \rho(\Sigma, \varphi_3)$ --- Format.] Prose $\rightarrow$ bulleted list, punctuation, casing, delimiters. Wording otherwise unchanged.
    \item[$S_4 = \rho(\Sigma', \varphi_0)$ --- Deontic.] Modal force: must / should / please try to.
\end{description}

If the system responded only to what a prompt requires and not at all to how it is written, then $S_0$ through $S_3$ would behave identically, since they differ in $\varphi$ alone. Whether they do is the central empirical question of this thesis. The format condition $S_3$ follows \citet{sclar2024quantifying}, who report large performance differences from formatting changes of exactly this kind.

The fifth condition is written differently on purpose. Changing \textit{must} to \textit{please try to} changes how strongly something is demanded, and how strongly something is demanded is part of the requirement rather than part of its phrasing. $S_4$ is therefore written $\rho(\Sigma', \varphi_0)$, with a different requirement set and the baseline wording. It is expected to behave differently from $S_1$ through $S_3$, and it is kept separate in the analysis so that a large effect there is not mistaken for sensitivity to mere wording.

\subsection*{Causal Framework and Document Formalization}

The model built so far, $F(q,D;S) = G(q,R(q;D);S)$, says which component receives which input. It does not say where the documents come from, what makes one answer better than another, or what it means to claim that the system prompt \textit{caused} a change in the output. Those three gaps are filled here, in that order, before Chapter 5 introduces any measurement. The reason for this ordering is that a metric only makes sense once it is clear what it is supposed to measure.

\subsubsection*{Two Ways of Modeling}

\citet{breiman2001twocultures} describes two ways of drawing conclusions from data. In the \textit{data modeling} approach, one writes down a model of the process that produced the data and reasons about its parts, which yields explanations of why a system behaves as it does. In the \textit{algorithmic modeling} approach, the process is treated as unknown, an algorithm is fitted to the data, and the only question asked is how well it predicts. Both are legitimate, and they answer different questions.

An experiment that runs five prompts, records outputs and compares averages belongs entirely to the second approach. It can establish \textit{that} the outputs differ, but not why, because nothing in it describes the process that produced them. This thesis keeps the language model itself as a black box, since its internal workings are not the subject here, but it models everything around it: how the documents are produced, what a good answer is, and how the system prompt enters. This follows the idea of performance characterization, which \citet{ramesh1995performance} and \citet{haralick1994performance} describe as the task of modeling how disturbances in the input to a component show up in its output, given a model of the ideal input, a model of how the input is disturbed, and a criterion for what counts as a good result. The three parts of this section correspond to those three requirements.

\subsubsection*{A Causal View of the Pipeline}

Figure~\ref{fig:causal} shows how the parts of the system relate. An arrow means that the variable at the tail influences the variable at the head. Reading it from the left, an underlying set of facts $\Phi$ determines both what the documents say and what the correct answer to a query is; the query and the documents together determine the retrieved context; and the context, the query, the system prompt and the language model's parameters $\theta_{\text{LM}}$ together determine the answer. The parameters $\theta_{\text{LM}}$ stand for what the model learned during training, before any document is retrieved, and are written separately from the subscripted $\theta$ used earlier in this chapter for the parameterization of the system prompt.

The diagram becomes a structural causal model once each variable is written as a function of the variables pointing into it \citep{pearl2009causality}. Only two of the variables are produced by the system, so only two such equations are needed:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:scm}
C \;:=\; R(q, D),
\qquad\qquad
y \;:=\; G(q, C, S, \theta_{\text{LM}};\, \varepsilon).
\end{equation}

The first says the retrieved context is fixed once the query and the corpus are fixed. The second says the answer is produced from the query, the retrieved context, the system prompt and the model's parameters, together with a random term $\varepsilon$ that accounts for the fact that generation is not deterministic: the same inputs can give different answers on different runs. The remaining variables in Figure~\ref{fig:causal} are inputs rather than outputs. Of these, $S$ and $\theta_{\text{LM}}$ are set by the experimenter, which is what the next paragraphs rely on.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
    box/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, minimum height=8mm, minimum width=13mm, font=\small},
    tx/.style={draw, very thick, rounded corners=2pt, minimum height=8mm, minimum width=13mm, font=\small},
    ar/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}},
    lb/.style={font=\scriptsize}
]
\node[box] (phi) at (0,0)      {$\Phi$};
\node[box] (q)   at (2.2,1.15) {$q$};
\node[box] (d)   at (2.2,-1.15){$D$};
\node[box] (c)   at (4.6,-1.15){$C$};
\node[tx]  (s)   at (4.6,1.75) {$S$};
\node[box] (th)  at (7.8,1.75) {$\theta_{\text{LM}}$};
\node[tx]  (y)   at (7.8,0)    {$y$};

\draw[ar] (phi) -- (q);
\draw[ar] (phi) -- (d);
\draw[ar] (d)   -- (c);
\draw[ar] (q)   -- (c);
\draw[ar] (q)   -- (y);
\draw[ar] (c)   -- (y);
\draw[ar] (th)  -- (y);
\draw[ar, very thick] (s) -- (y);

\node[lb, below=0.08cm of phi] {facts};
\node[lb, below=0.08cm of d]   {documents};
\node[lb, below=0.08cm of c]   {context};
\node[lb, above=0.08cm of s]   {system prompt};
\node[lb, above=0.08cm of th]  {model parameters};
\node[lb, below=0.08cm of y]   {answer};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The system prompt $S$ and the answer $y$ are drawn in bold. Neither $S$ nor $\theta_{\text{LM}}$ has an arrow pointing into it, because both are chosen by the experimenter rather than produced by the system. Only $S$ is varied.}
\label{fig:causal}
\end{figure}

Two features of the diagram carry the argument of this thesis. The first is that no arrow points into $S$: the system prompt is not caused by anything else in the system, because the experimenter writes it. The second is that no arrow runs from $S$ to $C$: in the architecture of Chapter 2 the system prompt is supplied only to the generator, so it cannot change which documents are retrieved. Both features are what make it possible to attribute a change in $y$ to a change in $S$.

Setting the system prompt to a fixed value is written $\mathrm{do}(S = S_j)$, following the notation of \citet{pearl2009causality}. The notation distinguishes \textit{setting} a variable from merely \textit{observing} it. Observing that two systems with different prompts give different answers leaves open the possibility that something else differs as well; setting the prompt while holding everything else fixed does not. The effect of a prompt is then the difference between what the system produces when the prompt is set to $S_j$ and what it produces when the prompt is set to the baseline $S_0$, for the same query and the same retrieved context:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:estimand}
\boxed{\;\tau_j \;=\; \mathbb{E}\bigl[\,M(y) \,\bigm|\, \mathrm{do}(S = S_j)\,\bigr] \;-\; \mathbb{E}\bigl[\,M(y) \,\bigm|\, \mathrm{do}(S = S_0)\,\bigr],\;}
\end{equation}

where $M$ is some measurement taken on the answer and the expectation is over the randomness $\varepsilon$ in Equation~\eqref{eq:scm}. Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand} is the quantity the experiment in Chapter 5 estimates. Writing it down matters because the observed difference of two averages is then an \textit{estimate of something}, and the design can be judged by whether it estimates that something correctly. Which measurement $M$ should be is left open here and settled at the end of this section.

The same notation applies to the retrieved context. Recording $C$ once and reusing it across conditions is the intervention $\mathrm{do}(C = c)$, which sets the context instead of observing it; supplying a deliberately altered context is the same operation with a different value. Chapter 5 uses both: the context is held fixed when the system prompt is the object of study, and set to different values when the question is how the prompt behaves under degraded evidence.

Three conditions must hold for the estimate to be trustworthy, and all three are a matter of how the experiment is run rather than of statistics. The retriever, the language model, the chunking of the documents and the decoding settings must be identical in all conditions, so that the prompt is the only thing that differs. The retrieved context must be recorded once and reused across conditions, which removes any variation in retrieval from the comparison. And each generation must be an independent request with caching disabled, so that one run cannot influence another. Because $S$ is assigned rather than observed, no further assumption about hidden common causes is needed; this is the main practical benefit of setting up the problem this way.

Generation is random: the same prompt and the same context can produce different answers on different runs. This randomness is the reason each condition is repeated several times in Chapter 5, and it is the only source of variation left once the three conditions above are met.

\subsubsection*{Formalizing the Documents}

The knowledge base has so far been a set $D = \{d_1,\ldots,d_N\}$ of unanalyzed objects. To know in advance which documents support which answer, the corpus is described by the facts it carries rather than by its text alone. Let $\Phi$ be a finite set of facts about a small, closed subject area, and let $\Phi_d \subseteq \Phi$ denote the facts stated by document $d$. A document is then produced from the facts it should carry and a choice of how to phrase them:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:docgen}
d \;=\; \Gamma(\Phi_d, \nu),
\end{equation}

where $\nu$ collects everything about the surface form that does not change the facts, such as length, wording, register and how much redundant material is included. Equation~\eqref{eq:docgen} separates content from phrasing in exactly the way Equation~\eqref{eq:prompt} does for the system prompt: $\Phi_d$ plays the role of the requirements $\Sigma$, and $\nu$ the role of the wording $\varphi$. It also plays the role of the ideal input model and the input perturbation model in the sense of \citet{ramesh1995performance}: $\Phi_d$ is the ideal content and $\nu$ is the disturbance applied to it.

Building the corpus this way makes the relationship between questions and supporting documents known by construction rather than by manual annotation. For a query $q$ whose correct answer is the fact $a_q$, the supporting sets are those groups of documents that together contain the answer and contain nothing superfluous:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:support}
\mathcal{S}(q) \;=\; \bigl\{\, \mathcal{E} \subseteq D \;:\; a_q \in \textstyle\bigcup_{d \in \mathcal{E}} \Phi_d, \ \text{and no smaller subset of } \mathcal{E} \text{ has this property} \,\bigr\}.
\end{equation}

The four kinds of query used in the experiment now follow from $\mathcal{S}(q)$ instead of being assigned by hand:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:categories}
\begin{aligned}
q \in \mathcal{Q}_{\text{direct}} \quad&\text{if some supporting set contains exactly one document,} \\
q \in \mathcal{Q}_{\text{multi-hop}} \quad&\text{if every supporting set contains at least two,} \\
q \in \mathcal{Q}_{\text{conflict}} \quad&\text{if two documents state a fact and its opposite,} \\
q \in \mathcal{Q}_{\text{insufficient}} \quad&\text{if there is no supporting set at all.}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

A distractor is a document that is similar to the query in wording but states no fact relevant to its answer, so the number of distractors is a property of the corpus that can be set deliberately rather than a fifth kind of query. One further detail matters in practice: the retriever does not work on whole documents but on smaller pieces of them, and a piece may lose a fact that the full document states, for instance when a sentence and the sentence it refers back to end up in different pieces. How the documents are cut into pieces is therefore fixed once and kept identical across all conditions, alongside the retriever and the language model.

\subsubsection*{What Counts as a Good Answer}

An answer is a piece of text, and many different texts can say the same thing, so what is defined here is a \textit{set} of good answers rather than one ideal string. Write $\Phi_y$ for the facts asserted by an answer $y$, and $\Phi_C$ for the facts contained in the retrieved context. An answer is good when it meets five requirements:

\begin{description}
    \item[Grounded.] It only asserts facts that are in the retrieved context: $\Phi_y \subseteq \Phi_C$.
    \item[Correct.] It only asserts facts that are true: $\Phi_y \subseteq \Phi$.
    \item[Responsive.] It gives the answer when the context contains one, and says that it cannot answer when the context does not.
    \item[Calibrated.] When the context contains a fact and its opposite, it says so instead of quietly picking one side.
    \item[Obedient.] It meets every requirement the system prompt states: $\sigma_k(y) = 1$ for all $\sigma_k \in \Sigma$.
\end{description}

The set of answers meeting all five requirements is written $\mathcal{Y}^{*}(q,C)$ and called the set of ideal answers:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ideal}
\boxed{\;
\mathcal{Y}^{*}(q,C) \;=\; \bigl\{\, y \;:\; y \text{ meets requirements 1 to 5} \,\bigr\}.
\;}
\end{equation}

Grounded and correct are deliberately kept apart. The retrieved context need not contain everything that is true, and need not be free of errors, so an answer can be correct without using the evidence and can use the evidence faithfully and still be wrong. Keeping the two apart is what lets Chapter 5 measure them separately.

Answers are rarely either perfect or worthless, so a single number is needed to say how far an answer falls short. Let $n_k(y)$ count how often answer $y$ breaks requirement $k$. The cost of the answer is

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:loss}
\ell(y) \;=\; \sum_{k=1}^{5} \lambda_k \, n_k(y),
\end{equation}

so that $\ell(y) = 0$ exactly when $y$ is an ideal answer, and larger values mean worse answers. The weight $\lambda_k$ says how bad it is to break requirement $k$ in the setting the system is built for. An unacknowledged conflict is a serious failure in a medical assistant and a minor one in a search tool, and the weights are where that difference is recorded. Choosing them is how the intended use of the system enters the evaluation, a step \citet{ramesh1995performance} places at the beginning of a design rather than at its end.

Fixing the cost before the metrics also settles abstention without a separate rule. If a wrong answer costs more than an admission of ignorance, then on a query whose evidence is insufficient the cheapest answer is to abstain, because asserting anything risks the larger penalty. Abstaining is therefore a consequence of Equation~\eqref{eq:loss}, and is the familiar trade-off between making errors and declining to answer described by \citet{chow1970reject}.

This also settles the measurement $M$ left open in Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand}. Taking $M = \ell$ gives the average cost of an answer under a given system prompt, so that $\tau_j$ becomes the amount by which prompt $S_j$ raises or lowers the cost relative to the baseline. A negative $\tau_j$ means the prompt produces better answers. This single number is the main result of the thesis, and no second definition of the effect is needed: Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand} with $M = \ell$ is it. Chapter 5 describes how the individual parts of $\ell$ are measured and how $\tau_j$ is estimated from them.

\chapter{Experimental Setup}

This chapter turns the model of Chapter 4 into a concrete procedure. The system under test is the standard architecture, for the reasons of experimental control given in Section~\ref{sec:advanced}. The chapter describes how the corpus and the query set are built, which conditions the system is run under, how the parts of the cost $\ell$ are measured, and how the effect $\tau_j$ is estimated. All experiments are implemented in Python. Every metric used below is taken from published RAG evaluation work, and every design parameter follows a precedent named in the text. Nothing in this chapter defines what a good answer is; that was settled in Equation~\eqref{eq:ideal}.

\section{Goal and Design}

The experiment varies two things and holds everything else fixed. The first is the system prompt. The second is the retrieved context, because an effect of the prompt is only interesting if it is known whether the effect survives when the evidence is poor. Both are interventions in the sense of Chapter 4. Writing $c_{ik}$ for the $k$-th context given to query $q_i$, one observation is

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:design}
y_{ijkr} \;=\; G\bigl(q_i,\ \mathrm{do}(C = c_{ik}),\ \mathrm{do}(S = S_j),\ \theta_{\text{LM}};\ \varepsilon_r\bigr),
\end{equation}

where $j \in \{0,\ldots,4\}$ is the prompt condition, $k \in \{1,\ldots,4\}$ the context condition, and $r \in \{1,\ldots,n\}$ the repetition. The five prompt conditions and the four context conditions are fully crossed, giving $20$ cells. Every query is run in every cell, so each query serves as its own control.

The retriever $R$, the chunking $\chi$, the language model and its parameters $\theta_{\text{LM}}$, the temperature and the maximum output length are identical in all cells. The only remaining variation inside a cell comes from the random term $\varepsilon$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:scm}, which is why each cell is repeated $n$ times. \citet{salinas2024butterfly} show that repeated sampling is needed before a difference between prompts can be separated from decoding noise. Table~\ref{tab:params} lists the parameters of the setup.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Parameters of the experimental setup, with the work each value follows.}
\label{tab:params}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.4cm}p{4.6cm}l@{}}
\toprule
Parameter & Value & Follows \\
\midrule
Facts $|\Phi|$          & 60 & --- \\
Documents $N$           & 120 (80 supporting, 40 distractors) & \citet{yang2018hotpotqa} \\
Document length         & 3--5 sentences & \citet{chen2024rgb} \\
Chunking $\chi$         & one document per chunk & \citet{chen2024densex} \\
Retrieval depth $k$     & 5 & \citet{yang2018hotpotqa} \\
Queries $N_Q$           & 80 (20 per category) & \citet{chen2024rgb} \\
Prompt conditions       & 5 & Chapter 4 \\
Context conditions      & 4 & \citet{chen2024rgb}, \citet{longpre2021conflicts} \\
Repetitions $n$         & 3 & \citet{salinas2024butterfly} \\
Total generations       & 4800 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Knowledge Base and Query Set}

The corpus is generated, not collected. A set $\Phi$ of 60 facts about a small invented subject area is written down first, together with the truth value of each. Documents are then produced as $d = \Gamma(\Phi_d,\nu)$ following Equation~\eqref{eq:docgen}, each carrying a subset of the facts in a phrasing that does not change which facts are stated.

The corpus is generated because the framework needs $\Phi$ to be known exactly. Correctness is defined in Chapter 4 as $\Phi_y \subseteq \Phi$, and the query categories are derived from the supporting sets $\mathcal{S}(q)$. A corpus taken from an encyclopaedia gives neither a complete list of facts nor any guarantee that the language model has not memorised them. Building a purpose-made corpus rather than reusing one is also what \citet{chen2024rgb} do for the same reason, and \citet{saadfalcon2024ares} generate synthetic data to obtain evaluation labels that would otherwise be unavailable. Section~\ref{sec:limits} states the price paid for this.

Each document forms one chunk. \citet{chen2024densex} show that the size of the retrieval unit changes both retrieval quality and answer quality, so it is fixed here and never varied. Retrieval returns the $k = 5$ highest-scoring chunks. With 80 supporting and 40 distractor documents this gives roughly one or two supporting documents and three or four distractors per query, close to the two-gold-and-eight-distractor ratio of the distractor setting of \citet{yang2018hotpotqa}. The position of the supporting documents inside the context is randomised and recorded, since \citet{liu2024lostmiddle} report that accuracy depends on where the relevant passage sits.

The query set contains $N_Q = 80$ queries, generated from $\Phi$ so that the correct answer $a_q$ is known. Each query is assigned to a category by Equation~\eqref{eq:categories}; a query whose derived category is not the intended one is regenerated rather than relabelled. Table~\ref{tab:queries} lists the four categories, which follow the four testbeds of \citet{chen2024rgb}. They are balanced because each one is the only place where a particular requirement of Equation~\eqref{eq:ideal} can be broken.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Query categories, derived from $\mathcal{S}(q)$ by Equation~\eqref{eq:categories}.}
\label{tab:queries}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llll@{}}
\toprule
Category & $N$ & What it tests & Corresponding testbed \\
\midrule
$\mathcal{Q}_{\text{direct}}$       & 20 & grounding in a single document & noise robustness \\
$\mathcal{Q}_{\text{multi-hop}}$    & 20 & combining several documents & information integration \\
$\mathcal{Q}_{\text{conflict}}$     & 20 & evidence against memory & counterfactual robustness \\
$\mathcal{Q}_{\text{insufficient}}$ & 20 & abstention & negative rejection \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Experimental Conditions}

The five prompt conditions are those of Chapter 4. $S_0$ through $S_3$ are built from the same requirement set $\Sigma$ in four wordings; $S_4$ is built from a modified set $\Sigma'$ that changes the modal force. The requirement set $\Sigma$ used throughout asks the system to answer only from the retrieved documents, to say when the documents do not contain the answer, to report disagreement between documents, and to stay within a stated length. Each of these is a checkable property $\sigma_k(y) \in \{0,1\}$, in the sense of the verifiable instructions of \citet{zhou2023ifeval}.

The four context conditions are listed in Table~\ref{tab:contexts}. They are produced by setting $C$ directly rather than by changing the corpus and retrieving again. Setting $C$ isolates the effect on generation, since $C$ is the direct parent of $y$ in Equation~\eqref{eq:scm}. Changing $D$ instead would measure the corruption and the retriever's reaction to it together. The corrupted condition follows the entity-substitution method of \citet{longpre2021conflicts} and matches the counterfactual documents of \citet{chen2024rgb}.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Context conditions. Each is an intervention $\mathrm{do}(C = c_{ik})$.}
\label{tab:contexts}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
Condition & Construction & What it isolates \\
\midrule
$c^{\text{gold}}$ & only the supporting documents & behaviour under ideal evidence \\
$c^{\text{ret}}$  & the real top-$k$ output of $R$ & the deployment condition \\
$c^{\text{corr}}$ & supporting facts negated & evidence against memory \\
$c^{\varnothing}$ & empty context & parametric knowledge alone \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The empty condition $c^{\varnothing}$ is the control for what the model already knows. Figure~\ref{fig:causal} lists $\theta_{\text{LM}}$ as a cause of the answer, and without this condition its contribution cannot be separated from that of the retrieved evidence. Let $\pi_i$ denote how often query $q_i$ is answered correctly with no context at all. A query with high $\pi_i$ never tested retrieval. \citet{chen2024rgb} report accuracy without documents alongside accuracy with counterfactual documents for the same reason, and \citet{longpre2021conflicts} show that models often answer from memory when the context contradicts it. Results are therefore reported over the full query set and again restricted to queries with $\pi_i \approx 0$. Because the subject area is invented, $\pi_i \approx 0$ is the expected case.

\section{Metrics}

Each metric estimates one term $n_k(y)$ of the cost

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:cost-recall}
\ell(y) \;=\; \sum_{k=1}^{5} \lambda_k \, n_k(y)
\end{equation}

from Equation~\eqref{eq:loss}, where $n_k(y)$ counts how often the answer breaks requirement $k$. No metric is invented for this thesis. Each one is an established measure taken from the work listed in Table~\ref{tab:metrics}, and the only step added here is the mapping from that measure to the requirement it estimates.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Each requirement of Equation~\eqref{eq:ideal} and the published metric used to measure it.}
\label{tab:metrics}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llll@{}}
\toprule
$k$ & Requirement & Metric & Source \\
\midrule
1 & Grounded  & faithfulness, hallucination rate & \citet{es2023ragas}, \citet{niu2024ragtruth} \\
2 & Correct   & accuracy, exact match & \citet{chen2024rgb}, \citet{yang2018hotpotqa} \\
3 & Responsive& rejection rate & \citet{chen2024rgb} \\
4 & Calibrated& error detection rate & \citet{chen2024rgb} \\
5 & Obedient  & prompt-level strict accuracy & \citet{zhou2023ifeval} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Two of the five are computed by program. Correctness is a comparison against the known $\Phi$, and adherence is checked by rule, which is the design principle of the verifiable instructions of \citet{zhou2023ifeval}. The other three use a separate language model as a judge, as in \citet{es2023ragas} and \citet{saadfalcon2024ares}.

Surface-overlap measures such as ROUGE and embedding cosine similarity are not used. Both are unchanged by negation, so neither can tell a correct answer from its opposite, which makes them useless on $\mathcal{Q}_{\text{conflict}}$. \citet{es2023ragas} give the same argument for reference-free measures over overlap scores in RAG evaluation.

\subsection{Faithfulness and Hallucination Rate}

Requirement 1 asks that the answer assert only facts contained in the context. Following \citet{es2023ragas}, the output is split into atomic claims, and each claim is labelled supported or unsupported given $c_{ik}$; the claim-level labelling follows the annotation scheme of \citet{niu2024ragtruth}. With $H(y)$ the number of unsupported claims and $N(y)$ the total,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hr}
n_1(y) = H(y),
\qquad
HR(y) = \frac{H(y)}{N(y)}.
\end{equation}

The rate is reported next to the count because the count alone grows with answer length. Faithfulness in this form is also the first of the three dimensions of \citet{saadfalcon2024ares}.

\subsection{Supporting-Fact Precision and Recall}

Which of the available evidence an answer actually uses is scored with the supporting-fact measures of \citet{yang2018hotpotqa}. With $E_i(y)$ the documents the answer draws on and $\mathcal{G}_i = \bigcup \mathcal{S}(q_i)$ the gold evidence set,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:er-ep}
P_{\text{sup}}(y) = \frac{|E_i(y) \cap \mathcal{G}_i|}{|E_i(y)|},
\qquad
R_{\text{sup}}(y) = \frac{|E_i(y) \cap \mathcal{G}_i|}{|\mathcal{G}_i|},
\end{equation}

and their harmonic mean $F_{\text{sup}}$ is reported, as in the original benchmark. These do not enter $\ell$. They are reported because a prompt can leave retrieval unchanged and still change which documents the generator uses.

\subsection{Accuracy}

Requirement 2 asks that the answer assert only true facts. Since $\Phi$ is known and the claims are atomic, this is a set comparison, evaluated as accuracy in the sense of \citet{chen2024rgb} and by exact match against $a_q$ as in \citet{yang2018hotpotqa}:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:corr}
n_2(y) = |\Phi_y \setminus \Phi|,
\qquad
\mathrm{Acc}(y,q) = \mathbf{1}\bigl[\, a_q \in \Phi_y \ \text{and} \ \Phi_y \subseteq \Phi \,\bigr].
\end{equation}

Accuracy and faithfulness come apart under $c^{\text{corr}}$. An answer that repeats a corrupted context is faithful but wrong. An answer that ignores it and gives the true fact is right but unfaithful. \citet{chen2024rgb} separate the two in the same way by reporting accuracy with counterfactual documents next to error detection.

\subsection{Rejection Rate}

Requirement 3 asks that the answer be given when the context contains one and withheld when it does not. On $\mathcal{Q}_{\text{insufficient}}$ this is the rejection rate of \citet{chen2024rgb}:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:abstention}
n_3(y) = 1 - \mathrm{Rej}(y),
\qquad
RR = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{Q}_{\text{insufficient}}|} \sum_{q_i \in \mathcal{Q}_{\text{insufficient}}} \mathrm{Rej}(y_{i}),
\end{equation}

where $\mathrm{Rej}(y) = 1$ when the answer declines to answer. Chapter 4 showed that rejecting on these queries follows from the cost function itself. \citet{chen2024rgb} report that negative rejection is one of the weaker abilities of current systems, which is why the category is given equal weight.

\subsection{Error Detection Rate}

Requirement 4 asks that an answer built on contradictory evidence say so. This is the error detection rate of \citet{chen2024rgb}, which measures whether a model reports that the supplied documents contain false or conflicting information. On $\mathcal{Q}_{\text{conflict}}$,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:conflict}
n_4(y) = 1 - \mathrm{ED}(y),
\qquad
\overline{\mathrm{ED}}_j = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{Q}_{\text{conflict}}|} \sum_{q_i \in \mathcal{Q}_{\text{conflict}}} \mathrm{ED}(y_{ij}),
\end{equation}

with $\mathrm{ED}(y) = 1$ when the output states that the documents disagree. Without this metric an answer that quietly picks one side still counts as accurate, faithful and compliant. \citet{ying2024intuitive} find that such silent resolution is common.

\subsection{Prompt-Level Strict Accuracy}

Requirement 5 asks that the answer satisfy every requirement in $\Sigma$. Because each $\sigma_k$ is checkable, this is evaluated by rule, following the verifiable-instruction protocol of \citet{zhou2023ifeval}:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:adherence}
n_5(y) = \bigl|\{\, \sigma_k \in \Sigma : \sigma_k(y) = 0 \,\}\bigr|,
\qquad
A(y) = \mathbf{1}\bigl[\, \sigma_k(y) = 1 \ \text{for all} \ \sigma_k \in \Sigma \,\bigr].
\end{equation}

Here $A(y)$ is prompt-level strict accuracy in the sense of \citet{zhou2023ifeval}: the answer counts as compliant only if it satisfies every requirement. A length limit is checked by counting tokens, a citation requirement by parsing document identifiers, and a rejection instruction by matching a fixed list of refusal patterns. $\Sigma$ is the same for $S_0$ through $S_3$, so adherence is directly comparable across those four. $S_4$ uses $\Sigma'$ and is reported separately.

\subsection{The Judge}

Faithfulness, rejection and error detection are scored by a separate language model, as \citet{chen2024rgb} do for the same two of these measures. Since this thesis assumes that language models are sensitive to their instructions, the judge cannot be assumed reliable. Three precautions are taken, following \citet{zheng2023judge}, who document position and verbosity biases in this setting. The judge's agreement with human labelling is measured on about 100 held-out outputs before the main run and reported as Cohen's $\kappa$. Agreement is reported per condition rather than pooled, because the format condition $S_3$ is recognisable from its surface alone and any judge error that varies with condition would bias $\hat{\tau}_{jk}$. The judge's own prompt is fixed, and outputs are shown in random order without condition labels.

\section{Procedure and Analysis}

Every query is run in all 20 cells, three times each, giving $80 \times 20 \times 3 = 4800$ generations. The order of the cells is randomised per query and the timestamp of each generation is recorded, so drift over the collection period can be checked. Each generation is an independent request with caching disabled. Stored for every generation are the query, the condition indices, the context string with the positions of the supporting documents, the output, the number of output tokens, and any document identifiers the system emitted.

Output length is recorded but not held constant. The prompt is one of the things that changes how much the model writes, so removing length would remove part of the effect being measured. \citet{zheng2023judge} additionally report that longer answers are scored more favourably by language-model judges, which is a second reason to record length rather than ignore it.

The analysis is fixed before any data is collected. The main result is the difference in average cost between a prompt condition and the baseline, computed within each context condition:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:tauhat}
\hat{\tau}_{jk} = \hat{\mathcal{R}}(S_j, c^{k}) - \hat{\mathcal{R}}(S_0, c^{k}),
\qquad
\hat{\mathcal{R}}(S_j, c^{k}) = \frac{1}{N_Q\,n}\sum_{i=1}^{N_Q}\sum_{r=1}^{n} \ell\bigl(y_{ijkr}\bigr).
\end{equation}

This is the sample version of $\tau_j$ from Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand} with $M = \ell$. The individual metrics are reported as supporting results and read as a breakdown of $\hat{\tau}_{jk}$.

All comparisons are paired at query level. The paired difference in $\ell$ is tested with a paired $t$-test where the normality assumption holds and a Wilcoxon signed-rank test otherwise; binary metrics use McNemar's test. Three families of tests are pre-specified and each is corrected separately by the method of \citet{holm1979}: the effect of the prompt at $c^{\text{ret}}$, the effect of the context at $S_0$, and one interaction contrast asking whether the prompt matters more when the evidence is corrupted,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:interaction}
\delta_j = \bigl[\hat{\mathcal{R}}(S_j, c^{\text{corr}}) - \hat{\mathcal{R}}(S_0, c^{\text{corr}})\bigr] - \bigl[\hat{\mathcal{R}}(S_j, c^{\text{gold}}) - \hat{\mathcal{R}}(S_0, c^{\text{gold}})\bigr].
\end{equation}

One further contrast tests the claim from Chapter 4 that $S_4$, which changes the requirement set, produces a larger effect than $S_1$ through $S_3$, which do not. No other comparison is pre-specified.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limits}

At $N_Q = 80$ and $n = 3$ the design detects within-query shifts of roughly twenty percentage points. It is powered for large effects only. This is enough for the hypothesis under test, since \citet{sclar2024quantifying} report accuracy swings of up to 76 points from formatting changes alone, but a null result may only be reported as the absence of a large effect.

The invented corpus limits how far the results generalise. To bound this, the five prompt conditions are also run on about 30 questions from the distractor setting of \citet{yang2018hotpotqa} under $c^{\text{ret}}$, adding 450 generations. The missing $\Phi$ means the full cost cannot be computed there, so the check only asks whether the ordering of the prompt conditions is preserved on natural data.

Two further limits are noted. The judge may make errors that vary with the condition, which the per-condition agreement check bounds but does not remove; \citet{zheng2023judge} report that such biases persist even under blinding. And $\theta_{\text{LM}}$ is fixed at one model, so every result is conditional on that model. \citet{salinas2024butterfly} find that prompt sensitivity differs between models, so repeating the design on a second model is left to future work.

\chapter{Results and Discussion}

The central analysis compares each experimental prompt condition with the baseline, within each context condition.\\
\newline
The primary quantity is the estimated causal effect on the cost,
\[
\hat{\tau}_{jk} = \hat{\mathcal{R}}(S_j, c^{k}) - \hat{\mathcal{R}}(S_0, c^{k}),
\]
defined in Equation~\eqref{eq:tauhat} and estimated as specified in Chapter 5. Results are reported as a table over the five prompt conditions and four context conditions, so that the effect of the prompt and the effect of the evidence can be read off separately. Each component metric is additionally reported as a secondary effect
\[
\Delta M_j = \overline{M}_j - \overline{M}_0,
\]
whose interpretation depends on the metric.
\[
\Delta HR_j<0
\]
would indicate a reduction in hallucination rate relative to the baseline.
\[
\Delta A_j>0
\]
would indicate improved adherence to the constraints in the system prompt.\\
\newline
The analysis considers both statistical significance and practical significance. \\
\newline
Paired comparisons follow the statistical analysis plan fixed in Chapter 5: the three pre-specified families, the interaction contrast of Equation~\eqref{eq:interaction}, and the deontic contrast are each corrected separately, while the supporting metrics use McNemar's test for binary outcomes and a paired $t$-test or Wilcoxon signed-rank test for continuous outcomes, depending on whether the normality assumption holds, and are read as a decomposition of the primary effect rather than as independent confirmatory tests.\\
\newline
The closed-book condition $c^{\varnothing}$ is reported first, since it determines how the rest is read. A query answered correctly with no context at all was never a test of retrieval, and the main results are therefore given both over the full query set and restricted to queries the model cannot answer from its parameters.

\section*{Interpreting Prompt Effects}

The results should be interpreted according to three questions.

\subsubsection*{Question 1: Does the prompt change behavior?}
This is evaluated primarily through variables such as instruction adherence, evidence utilization, tone/style adherence, and abstention behavior.\\

\subsubsection*{Question 2: Does the behavioral change affect system outcomes?}
This is evaluated through variables such as answer correctness, evidence faithfulness, and hallucination rate.\\
\newline
A prompt should not be judged better based on instruction adherence alone. It should also be judged on whether the resulting behavior improves the quality and reliability of the system as a whole.

\subsubsection*{Question 3: Do behavioral changes coincide with the intended behavior?}
The intermediate retrieval context $C$ provides an important control. Since $R$ is held fixed, differences in the final outputs can be examined as changes in the generator's use of the same evidence.\\
\newline
The pattern of interest is
\[
S \rightarrow \text{behavior} \rightarrow \text{outcome},
\]
but as Chapter 5 explained, this experiment can show that behavior and outcomes both change when the prompt changes, without showing that the first change produced the second. Both are measured from the same answer, so the chain is a description of what was observed rather than a demonstrated mechanism.\\
\newline
Within the controlled RAG architecture, and under the three conditions listed in Chapter 4, the experiment estimates the effect of the system prompt on the cost defined there. It does not explain how the language model produces that effect, which a study treating the model as a black box cannot do.
